From 1ab167a0ac0ff076eb73a24d2b88a94a0f51f1e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 13:49:35 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: report the guest's architecture and carry the
 transport little endian on every guest

A host that decodes more than one architecture must learn which one a guest
is before it reads a record. ARCH_INFO answers with the ELF machine, class
and byte order, the page size, the ABIs system call records may carry, the
number of the first native system call, the native table's size and identity
(the SHA-256 of the protocol file generated with it, which the generator now
also writes into <asm/antinomie_syscalls.h>), the user address width, the
debug engine's capability flags and the prefix of every layout name. It is
contract revision 20; a host asks only a guest whose INFO revision is 20 or
later, because an older guest counts an unknown opcode as a bad command.

The shared-memory transport keeps its layout. A 64-bit little-endian guest
still announces ABI 9 and its words are native accesses, as before. Any other
guest announces ABI 10: every field little endian (message headers too), and
on a 32-bit guest, which has no 64-bit atomics, the POS32 profile: each
moving 64-bit word (queue position, heartbeat, nonce, sequence, counter)
travels as its low 32 bits, positions run modulo 2^32 and every reader widens
one next to a position it knows. queue.h takes the profile, guest.c reaches
every shared field through accessors that expand to the old expressions on a
64-bit little-endian guest.

Process cores of a 32-bit guest are flagged CORE_NATIVE_ELF32 instead of
CORE_NATIVE_ELF64, and the debug CAPS flags gain SW_SINGLE_STEP and
READ_WATCH for the architectures that need them. x86-64 reports neither and
its shared-ring bytes do not change.
